## DESCRIPTION
## Write the equations given an augmented coefficient matrix.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## KEYWORDS('linear system','augmented coefficient matrix')

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Systems of linear equations)
## DBsection(Augmented matrices)
## Date(September 5, 2012)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)

# updated 2023.11.10

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',             'PGML.pl',
    'parserImplicitEquation.pl', 'parserMultiAnswer.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("ImplicitEquation");
Context()->variables->add(z => 'Real');

$A = Matrix([ map { [ map { random(-6, 6) } (1 .. 4) ] } 1 .. 3 ]);

@eqns = (
    map {
        ImplicitEquation(
            $A->element($_, 1) . 'x+'
                . $A->element($_, 2) . 'y+'
                . $A->element($_, 3) . 'z='
                . $A->element($_, 4),
            limits         => [ [ -15, 15 ], [ -15, 15 ], [ -15, 15 ] ],
            ImplicitPoints => 20
        )->reduce
    } (1 .. 3)
);

BEGIN_PGML
Consider the augmented coefficient matrix:

[``` [$A] ```]

Write the matrix as a linear system below.  (Use the variables [`x`], [`y`], and [`z`].)

- [_]{$eqns[0]}
- [_]{$eqns[1]}
- [_]{$eqns[2]}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For each row of the matrix, use the elements as coefficients of the equation and the right hand side for the last column.  These are

[```\begin{aligned}
[$eqns[0]] \\
[$eqns[1]] \\
[$eqns[2]]
\end{aligned} ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
